  "answer": "No, the Aussie dollar isn't weak against the yen. It's come down a bit from its recent peak, but it's still high compared with the last few years.\n\n**Where it is now:** A$1 bought **¥109.67** at the RBA's 4pm reading on **16 October 2026**, the latest figure available.\n\n**Recent moves:**\n- **Since late August:** it's down about 4%. The month-end high was ¥114.54 in August 2026, and it peaked at ¥114.63 on 1 September before sliding to about ¥109–110. Through October it has stayed in a narrow ¥109.4–110.7 range.\n- **Over the past 18 months:** it's still up about 20% from ¥91.43 at the end of April 2025. It rose steadily through late 2025 and early 2026, from about ¥100.8 in October 2025 to ¥110.9 in February 2026 and above ¥114 in April–May 2026.\n- **Since 2023:** today's rate is near the top of the range. The lowest month-end rate was ¥89.10 (March 2023), and the 2024 peak was ¥106.61 (June 2024). Most of 2023–2025 sat between ¥90 and ¥100.\n\n**Caveat:** this data only goes back to January 2023, so I can't compare against older averages here.\n\n**In short:** the dollar has lost some ground since its late-August/early-September high. But at about ¥110 it still buys more yen than at almost any point from 2023 to 2025, so for things like a trip to Japan it's still a good rate by recent standards.",
